ID: Ch11a-Intro
TEXT:

 

"You're the stillwater who leveled Olmnar with a flick of his fingers?" I ask Eiden.

"I am indeed," Eiden says.

"Wasn't that twenty years ago?" I say. "Just how old are you?"

Eiden tilts his head in mild contemplation.

"To be honest, I lost count a few centuries back," he says. "The calendar has changed at least five times since I was born, so I have no idea what year it would be by the old reckoning. Early six hundreds is my best estimate."

"You're... over six centuries old?"

"That is what I just told you," Eiden says. "Do try to keep up."

"How could you?" Flower's voice trembles, a single tear cutting down her cheek. "How could you kill all those people? What did anyone in Olmnar ever do to you?!"

"To me?" Eiden says. "Nothing at all. But the people of Olmnar were the first to hunt animals for sport. The entire war between beasts and men traces back to them. They needed to be punished."

"Punished?" Red flares behind Flower's eyes, hot and sudden. "All of them? The old people? The children? Who gave you the right?! If anyone had that right, it was Eleya, not you!"

"Ah, the old fox!" Eiden snaps his fingers. "I almost forgot why I dropped in."

He turns toward me, hands tucked into his loose sleeves.

"Barry, did you know the golden fox placed a tracking spell on your group before you left the ruins?"

"A tracking spell?" I say. "Why?"

"No idea!" Eiden says with a bright shrug. "Though I suspect she finds you rather interesting as well."

He traces his index finger through the empty air, tapping at invisible points in space. A warm golden radiance blooms around us, coalescing into the translucent head of the giant fox.

"Eiden?" Eleya's voice echoes from the projection. "Eiden, is that you?"

"Hello, old friend," Eiden says. "Miss me?"

"Eiden, what are you doing?" the fox demands. "Stop this at once!"

The stillwater ignores her entirely, plucking at the intricate weave until he catches a thread of solid golden light between two fingers.

"Eiden!" Eleya's voice turns sharp. "Don't you dare—"

Eiden offers a cheerful little wave and yanks the thread. The light shatters with a metallic chime, and the fox's image vanishes into nothing.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch11a-Intro2
TEXT:

 "Did you break it?" I ask.

"Broken for now, though it will re-anchor if you linger in one spot," Eiden says. "I believe I heard you mention Thilias earlier. Allow me to offer a lift. Free of charge."

He snaps his fingers.

My ribcage collapses inward, the world pinching down to a single dimensionless needle-point. Senses, breath, and balance drop away in one sickening instant—and then I stumble forward onto hard-packed dirt, gasping for air in front of a towering curtain of dressed stone that stretches past both horizons.

Beside me, Rose steadies herself against her knees, her coughing fit finally subsiding as she looks up at the battlements.

"Rose, is this..."

"Thilias," she breathes before I can finish. "He wasn't lying. He dropped us right at the gates."

"Rose!" Daren calls out, jogging toward us.

The paralysis has worn off; Kate and Hadrik are already on their feet, dusting themselves off as they close the distance.

"Rose, are you hurt?" Daren asks, scanning her face. "Do you need a healing spell?"

"No, I'm all right," Rose says. "Just caught my breath wrong. Nothing broken."

I turn toward the golden pendant resting against Flower's chest. "Arraka. Do you feel Eiden anywhere nearby? Is he floating around invisible?"

"No, he stayed behind," Arraka's voice drifts from the metal.

"Will you warn us if he sneaks up on us again?" I ask.

"Sure. Why not?"

"Just like that?" I say, narrowing my eyes.

"Just like that," Arraka says. "Any chance to ruin Eiden's fun is time well spent."

"So you two have a history?"

"Oh, we go way back," she says with a dry rasp.

Kate steps up beside Flower, eyeing the red streaks on the girl's cheeks. "Flower... are you all right?"

"I'm fine," Flower mutters, scrubbing her sleeve across her eyes.

"If you need to talk—"

"I said I'm FINE!" Flower snaps, rounding on her.

choice(""Oh, yes, you definitely look fine to me!"", Ch11a-Definitely, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Definitely)
choice(""Maybe we should take a small break before we enter the city."", Ch11a-Break, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Break)
choice(""She says she's fine, so let's leave her alone for now."", Ch11a-Alone, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Alone)


ID: Ch11a-Definitely
TEXT:

 "Oh, yes, you definitely look fine to me!"

Flower glares at me, her lower lip trembling.

"I'm not stupid," she says. "I know you're mocking me. I know all of us put together wouldn't scratch Eiden if he got serious. I know that! But I couldn't just stand there and do nothing!"

"If it helps," Daren says, stepping between us with a wry grimace, "I did the exact same thing the first time I ran into him. And I didn't even have a personal score to settle."

Flower looks up. "You did?"

"Charged him like a bull with my shield raised," Daren says. "He put me to sleep with a flick of his wrist before I crossed half the distance."

"Halt, there!" a harsh voice calls from the gatehouse. A city guard marches out in full leather and iron, spear lowered. "State your business! Identify yourselves!"

Rose steps forward with an easy, practiced smile. "It's all right, Ralph! They're with me."

A glowing glyph pulses in my field of vision, warning me that the guard is a protected local. A second warning flashes over his partner standing watch by the portcullis.

"Rose! By the gods, you're back," Ralph says, dropping his spear to rest on his boot. "The oildrip broke containment while you were out in the wilds. They've quarantined the entire Sparrow's district."

He glances across our group, taking in Daren's plate armor and Hadrik's broad shoulders.

"I take it these are the travelers who saw you home safely. You have my thanks, all of you. Thilias wouldn't be the same without our herbalist."

"Is there word from the clinics, Ralph?" Rose asks.

"Nurse Martha left strict orders to find her the minute you passed the gates," Ralph says. "Check in at her tent before you head anywhere else."

"Understood," Rose says. "I'll drop my guests at the house and head straight down. Thank you, Ralph."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Luxurious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Luxurious)


ID: Ch11a-Break
TEXT:

 "Maybe we should take a small break before we enter the city."

"We have time," Rose says. "I'm days ahead of schedule thanks to that jump. An extra hour won't hurt."

"Hear that, Flower?" I turn toward her. "We can just—"

Flower is already ten paces ahead, her small bare feet kicking up dust as she heads straight for the archway.

"Hey! Wait up," I say, jogging after her.

"Leave me alone," she mutters without turning.

"Flower, come on—"

"Halt, there!" a guard barks from the gatehouse, stepping into our path with a leveled spear. "State your business! Identify yourselves!"

Rose catches up, breathless but smiling. "It's all right, Ralph! They're with me."

A translucent prompt blinks in my vision, identifying the guard as a local off-limits to tournament violence. A matching warning hovers over the sentry beside the iron portcullis.

"Rose! By the gods, you're back," Ralph says, lowering his weapon. "The oildrip broke containment while you were out in the wilds. They've quarantined the entire Sparrow's district."

He looks us over with open relief.

"I take it these are the travelers who saw you home safely. You have my thanks, all of you. Thilias wouldn't be the same without our herbalist."

"Is there word from the clinics, Ralph?" Rose asks.

"Nurse Martha left strict orders to find her the minute you passed the gates," Ralph says. "Check in at her tent before you head anywhere else."

"Understood," Rose says. "I'll drop my guests at the house and head straight down. Thank you, Ralph."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Luxurious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Luxurious)


ID: Ch11a-Alone
TEXT:

 "She says she's fine, so let's leave her alone for now."

"I suppose..." Kate says, her gaze lingering on Flower with quiet tension.

"The gate is right ahead," Daren cuts in, projecting his voice with forced cheer. "Come on. We've made it."

"Halt, there!" a guard calls from the gatehouse, marching out with spear in hand. "State your business! Identify yourselves!"

Rose steps forward with an easy, reassuring smile. "It's all right, Ralph! They're with me."

A warning sigil flares in my vision: local resident, untouchable by tournament mages. The sentry flanking the iron portcullis carries the same mark.

"Rose! By the gods, you're back," Ralph says, planting his spear butt in the dirt. "The oildrip broke containment while you were out in the wilds. They've quarantined the entire Sparrow's district."

He looks across our ragged band.

"I take it these are the travelers who saw you home safely. You have my thanks, all of you. Thilias wouldn't be the same without our herbalist."

"Is there word from the clinics, Ralph?" Rose asks.

"Nurse Martha left strict orders to find her the minute you passed the gates," Ralph says. "Check in at her tent before you head anywhere else."

"Understood," Rose says. "I'll drop my guests at the house and head straight down. Thank you, Ralph."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Luxurious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Luxurious)


ID: Ch11a-Luxurious
TEXT:

 Ralph signals his partner, and the heavy iron-studded gates groan inward on greased hinges.

Instead of marble villas and polished plazas, Thilias welcomes us with leaning timber shacks, stagnant mud, and a stench thick enough to choke a horse. Open gutters run black through the center of the street. Crowds in patched homespun shuffle past, hollow-eyed and wary, parting around us in silence. Above every bent head, the tournament sigil burns in faint blue light, warning me that these people are strictly off-limits.

"Pardon my ignorance, Rose," Daren says as we pick our way through the muck, "but aren't Varathian cities supposed to be the crown jewel of civilization?"

"They are," Rose says. "This is the Beggar's district. We came through the eastern gate. The nobility and the merchant princes use the western avenue, and they rarely cross into these quarters."

"You divide your towns by wealth?" Daren asks, incredulous.

"Of course," Rose says. "Is it not like that on the eastern continent?"

"No," Daren says, his brow furrowing. "Segregating the destitute only breeds resentment and open revolt."

"In Varathia, the poor don't revolt," Rose says quietly. "A single insult to a noble gets an entire family put to death."

choice(""Their families? Aren't there laws against this sort of thing?"", Ch11a-Families, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Families)
choice(""Your nobles are starting to sound less like nobles, and more like a gang of thugs."", Ch11a-Thugs, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Thugs)


ID: Ch11a-Families
TEXT:

 "Their families? Aren't there laws against this sort of thing?"

"There are," Rose says. "The nobility simply isn't bound by them."

"Monstrous," Daren mutters, his jaw tightening. "The cruelty these people must endure every single day... How can any ruler permit it?"

"The royalty and the high lords write the codes," Rose says with a helpless shrug. "They see no reason to alter what serves them. You truly come from another world, don't you?"

Flower clamps both hands over her mouth with a sharp gasp.

"Oh gods!" she blurts. "I forgot! I need a new chain for Arraka's amulet. Will you wait here? I saw a smithy back by the gatehouse."

"Make it quick," Hadrik grunts, scanning the rotting eaves. "I don't want to linger in this gutter longer than we have to. Place turns my stomach."

Flower scampers back toward the gate. Hadrik stretches his shoulders, eyes wandering the narrow street until they snag on a hanging iron shingle.

"Hey, Daren," Hadrik calls out. "Armor smith right on the corner. Want to take a look? Might be your chance to trade that battered breastplate for something decent."

"I'll pass," Daren says, his tone like vinegar.

"Suit yourself," Hadrik says with a grin, disappearing through the heavy oak door.

Kate glances at Daren. "You two really don't get along, do you?"

"I have no idea what you mean," Daren says stiffly. "Hadrik and I get along fine."

"Right," Kate says.

A girl's high, ragged shriek cuts through the rumble of the street.

Ten paces to our right, in the middle of the thoroughfare, a thickset man in embroidered silk and heavy gold chains is kicking a teenage girl in the ribs.

Her clothes are coarse, sun-bleached burlap, smeared with gutter mud. What turns my stomach is the complete absence of fight in her: she curls into a ball, forearms locked around her head, offering no resistance, no scramble to escape, no second cry for help. Townsfolk walk right past, eyes fixed firmly on the cobblestones as though stepping around horse droppings.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Slave, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Slave)


ID: Ch11a-Thugs
TEXT:

 "Your nobles are starting to sound less like nobles, and more like a gang of thugs."

Rose casts an anxious glance at the nearest passersby, her voice dropping to a whisper. "I wouldn't say that where anyone can hear..."

"Why?" I ask. "They'll throw me in a dungeon for stating the obvious?"

"Yes, actually," Rose says. "You really do live in a different world."

Flower clamps both hands over her mouth with a sharp gasp.

"Oh gods!" she blurts. "I forgot! I need a new chain for Arraka's amulet. Will you wait here? I saw a smithy back by the gatehouse."

"Make it quick," Hadrik grunts, scanning the rotting eaves. "I don't want to linger in this gutter longer than we have to. Place turns my stomach."

Flower scampers back toward the gate. Hadrik stretches his shoulders, eyes wandering the narrow street until they snag on a hanging iron shingle.

"Hey, Daren," Hadrik calls out. "Armor smith right on the corner. Want to take a look? Might be your chance to trade that battered breastplate for something decent."

"I'll pass," Daren says, his tone like vinegar.

"Suit yourself," Hadrik says with a grin, disappearing through the heavy oak door.

Kate glances at Daren. "You two really don't get along, do you?"

"I have no idea what you mean," Daren says stiffly. "Hadrik and I get along fine."

"Right," Kate says.

A girl's high, ragged shriek cuts through the rumble of the street.

Ten paces to our right, in the middle of the thoroughfare, a thickset man in embroidered silk and heavy gold chains is kicking a teenage girl in the ribs.

Her clothes are coarse, sun-bleached burlap, smeared with gutter mud. What turns my stomach is the complete absence of fight in her: she curls into a ball, forearms locked around her head, offering no resistance, no scramble to escape, no second cry for help. Townsfolk walk right past, eyes fixed firmly on the cobblestones as though stepping around horse droppings.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Slave, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Slave)


ID: Ch11a-Slave
TEXT:

 "What is wrong with these people?" Daren's voice shakes with raw outrage. "They act like this is normal. I'm not standing by while that bloated swine butchers her. I'm putting him in the dirt."

"Daren, stop!" Rose grabs his armored forearm with both hands. "You can't! That man is kin to one of the kings of Varathia, and that girl is his slave. She is his legal property!"

"She is his... what?" Daren looks at her, the color draining from his face.

Rose flinches under the cold fury in his eyes.

"She's a slave," Rose whispers. "She has no legal standing. No rights. Under the law, he can use her however he pleases, and discard her whenever he likes."

Daren stares at Rose, stunned into silence.

"You're telling me this is lawful?" Daren demands, his voice rising. "Treating a human being like cattle? Do you find this acceptable, Rose?!"

Rose parts her lips, but nothing comes out.

"Whatever I think of it," she says at last, her voice trembling, "tournament law still protects him. You saw what happened to the mage who raised a hand against me in the woods. You'll be disqualified and banished before your blade clears the scabbard."

Daren's fist clenches around the grip of his sword, his leather glove creaking under the strain. He offers no retort. He simply turns his back on her, staring at the horror in the street.

"I'm sorry," the girl whimpers from the mud as the man drives a heel into her shoulder. "Please, master, I'm sorry!"

"I don't need your whining, you useless wretch," the nobleman spits, looking down at her swollen, bloodied face. "I bought a servant, not dead weight. If you can't carry a few parcels, what good are you?"

Beside the girl lie ten massive sacks of flour, dried meat, and grain—easily twice her body weight.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Favor, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Favor)


ID: Ch11a-Favor
TEXT:

 "Master, please!" the girl begs from her knees. "I had no sleep in the holding pens, and I haven't rested a moment since you bought me this morning. If you grant me only an hour to catch my breath—"

The nobleman grabs a fistful of her hair, yanking her head back until her neck strains.

"So it's my fault you're useless?" he sneers. "Is that it?"

"N-no, master!" she gasps, trembling violently. "Never—"

"Perhaps I should slit your throat right here and buy a proper worker," the man says, resting a heavy hand near his belt. "One that carries bags. One that holds its tongue. Ten silvers was pocket change anyway."

"Please!" Tears cut through the grime on her cheeks. "I won't speak again! I'll carry them! Just give me another chance!"

"Do you see now, Daren?" Eiden's voice floats from the air behind us.

Daren whips around, his face hardening as he spots the stillwater hovering inches above the cobbles, eyes closed, hands tucked loosely into his sleeves.

"Do you see the true face of the humanity you are so desperate to protect?" Eiden says. "Look at them. Any one of these good citizens could step forward, break that fool's neck, and walk away. But they won't. Some claim they are obeying the law. Others say they must shield their families. But you and I know the truth: they look away because it is convenient. Because lying to themselves is easier than facing the rot."

Beside me, Rose lowers her gaze, staring silently at the filthy stones.

"You aren't like them, are you, Daren?" Eiden continues smoothly. "You would have broken that noble in half the moment his heel touched her ribs—if not for the tournament's ward tying your hands."

"Be silent," Daren growls.

"A pity, isn't it?" Eiden smiles. "Forced to watch, bound by one meager charm. What if I told you I can unbind it?"

"You're insane," Daren says.

Eiden chuckles. "You needn't take my word. Once I grant it, you'll feel the ward dissolve. You have only to ask. Ask me for the power, Daren, and I will give it freely. No price. No conditions."

"I will never ask you for a favor!" Daren snaps.

"No?" Eiden tilts his head. "Then you are content to leave the girl to her butcher and walk on, exactly like the rest."

Daren's jaw works, teeth grinding, but no words come.

choice("Ask Eiden to give me the power instead, so I can save the girl.", Ch11a-Power, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Power, v_ch11_saved_slave = 1)
choice("Say nothing.", Ch11a-Nothing, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Nothing)


ID: Ch11a-Nothing
TEXT:

 "Well, Daren?" Eiden asks after a long pause. "What will it be?"

"I'll prove you wrong," Daren says, voice low and raw. "There is decency in every man. Even in someone as vile as this noble."

He turns and marches straight toward them. The girl is frantically hauling at the heavy sacks, her thin arms shaking as they tumble back into the mud. The nobleman raises a heavy backhand to strike her again.

"Hold!" Daren's voice booms down the alley as he closes the distance. "You're hurting her!"

The nobleman stops, blinking at Daren's plate armor as if confronted by a talking dog.

"What are you, a street clown?" the man asks. "Do you charge coin to announce the obvious? What next? Will you inform me the sun rises in the east?"

"She is a human being!" Daren steps into his space. "She bleeds as you bleed. She feels pain, terror, exhaustion. How can you treat another person like dirt beneath your boots?"

"She is not a person, outsider," the nobleman sneers. "She is a slave. An existence lower than vermin. Do you weep for every fly you swat?"

"Have you no eyes?!" Daren demands. "Look at her!"

"It is you who are blind," the nobleman says. "I realize abstract concepts elude a barbarian, but allow me to educate you."

He grabs the girl by her hair and drags her upright, forcing her to face Daren.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Civilization, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Civilization)


ID: Ch11a-Civilization
TEXT:

 "This is a slave," the nobleman says. "The lowest tier of human order. In a civilized realm, each caste serves its purpose. The base exist to shoulder labor so the enlightened minds above them may govern, invent, and thrive. Slaves built the very walls that keep you safe. They dug the sewers, raised the stone keeps, paved every road you walk on. Slaves are the bedrock of Varathian greatness. Your inability to grasp so basic a truth only proves how crude your homeland truly is."

"That isn't civilization!" Daren shouts. "It's butchery! Treating living souls as disposable tools! It isn't too late—you can still choose mercy. Listen to reason!"

The nobleman sighs, shaking his head. "Words are wasted on your kind. If speech is too complex for a savage, a visual demonstration will have to suffice."

He reaches inside his embroidered coat and pulls out a broad-bladed skinning knife.

Daren's breath catches. "Wait. What are you doing?"

"Showing you what happens to broken property," the man says smoothly. "When I carve out her throat, nothing will change. No tears will fall, no crowd will gather, the city will not pause for half a second. The world moves on as if she never drew breath."

"Hold!" Daren lunges forward, hands up. "I spoke out of turn—put the blade away! We can talk, negotiate—please!"

The girl's eyes go completely hollow. She offers no struggle. She stares blankly at the blood-stained mud, waiting for the steel.

Eiden murmurs into my ear: "Look at that. The holy healer is about to talk that poor thing straight into an early grave. Are you satisfied with this, Barry?"

choice("Ask Eiden for the power to attack the noble, so I can save the girl.", Ch11a-Power, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Power, v_ch11_saved_slave = 2)
choice("Stay silent.", Ch11a-Silent, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Silent, v_ch11_saved_slave = 3)


ID: Ch11a-Silent
TEXT:

 I hold my tongue, staring straight ahead as if Eiden weren't there.

Eiden's smile sharpens. He turns to Kate.

"And you, Kate?" he asks. "The offer stands. Ask, and I strip the ward from you right now. Will you leave her to bleed?"

Kate looks away, her jaw rigid, hands clenched inside her cloak. "It isn't... our concern. We can't save everyone."

"How wonderfully pragmatic," Eiden says.

"I have gold!" Daren's voice cracks in desperation. "Hundreds of coins! I'll pay ten times what you bought her for!"

"I have vaults of gold, outsider," the nobleman says. "I would rather give you an education. You'll thank me in time."

"Please, just hear me—"

With a casual flick of his wrist, the nobleman draws the blade across her throat.

Blood sprays across the cobblestones. When he lets go of her hair, she crumples like a dropped sack into the muck, dark crimson pooling around her neck.

"NO!" Daren roars, dropping to his knees in the mud.

He presses his palms against her neck, flooding the wound with blazing white light. "No, no, no, no, no..."

The light flickers and dies against cold flesh. Tears cut through the grime on Daren's face as he gathers the limp body into his arms, crushing her against his breastplate. Rose covers her mouth, mortified, while Kate turns her back, shoulders tense.

"Weeping over discarded refuse," the nobleman chuckles. "A lost cause, truly. I pity you."

Daren goes utterly still. He lays the girl gently on the stones and stands up. When he turns, all color has drained from his face, leaving only flat, dead fury.

"Eiden," he whispers. "Give me the power."

"Are you certain?" Eiden asks.

"You said free of charge," Daren says. "Give it to me."

Eiden raises his hands. A sharp gust rips through the alley, spinning in a tight vortex around him for a heartbeat before vanishing.

"It is done," Eiden says.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Wonderful, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Wonderful)


ID: Ch11a-Wonderful
TEXT:

 Daren draws his broadsword in a single blinding arc.

Steel flashes. The nobleman's head flies from his shoulders before the man can blink, hitting the dirt with a wet thud as his body pitches forward into the mud.

The entire street freezes. Passersby stop dead, staring in petrified silence at the headless corpse.

Clear, melodic laughter shatters the quiet.

"Wonderful!" Eiden laughs, drifting upward on a breeze. "Magnificent! Let this image burn into your soul, healer. Let this mark the first step on your road to despair. Never forget the day you chose your holy pride over an innocent life."

Daren lets out an unearthly roar and lunges, his sword slicing through empty air as Eiden rises effortlessly out of reach.

Eiden looks down at Kate and me.

"One final detail before I depart. The spell I wove was an area enchantment, not a single target. The two of you carry the exact same gift as your pious friend. If you doubt me, picture striking any soul on this street. The tournament ward will offer no resistance whatsoever. The effect is permanent. Use it as you see fit. Farewell!"

He snaps his fingers—and vanishes without a sound.

I test the thought, imagining a strike against a nearby bystander. The suffocating psychic resistance that usually clamps down on violent intent is completely gone.

Daren wipes his blade clean on a scrap of cloth, sheathes it, and turns toward us. His eyes are hollow.

"We're done here," he says quietly. "Find Hadrik and Flower. We're leaving."

"Daren, I..." Rose reaches out, but Daren walks past her without a glance, heading straight for the armor shop.

Hadrik steps out as Daren approaches, looking disappointed. "Why the rush? I thought we were waiting for Flower."

"Something happened," Rose says. "We're taking a different route to the house. We'll explain when we get there. Let's find Flower."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Modest, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Modest)


ID: Ch11a-Power
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 1) {
 "If Daren won't accept the power, then I will," I say.

"I expected as much," Eiden says, his smile widening. "Very well."

He raises his palms. A sharp draft spirals around him, lifting his loose tunic, then settles into dead calm.

"It is done," he says.

"Strange," I mutter. "I don't feel any different."

"Look at the nobleman," Eiden says. "Imagine striking him. You will find the tournament ward offers no resistance."

I test the thought, picturing a fist to the man's throat. The psychic clamp that usually suffocates violent intent is completely gone.

I sprint across the cobbles, cock my right fist, and drive a hard punch straight into the nobleman's jaw. The impact sends him sprawling onto his back in the mud.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 2) {
 "Fine," I say. "Give me the power. I'll save the girl."

"Very well!" Eiden says with an airy wave.

He raises his palms. A sharp draft spirals around him, lifting his loose tunic, then settles into dead calm.

"It is done," he says.

"Strange," I mutter. "I don't feel any different."

"Look at the nobleman," Eiden says. "Imagine striking him. You will find the tournament ward offers no resistance."

I test the thought, picturing a blow to the man's throat. The suffocating psychic resistance never arrives.

I sprint across the cobbles, cock my right fist, and smash him across the jaw. He crashes backward into the muck, the skinning knife slipping from his fingers.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 1 || v_ch11_saved_slave == 2) {
 "What is the meaning of this?!" the nobleman howls from the dirt, spitting blood. "How dare you?! Do you know who I am?!"

"Sure," I tell him. "Just another rich jackass who let his title rot his brain. Welcome to the real world."

"You think you can act cocky because you slipped past the ward?" he snarls, struggling up onto one elbow. "You have no idea what you've stepped into, boy. Disqualification will be the least of your concerns when my house is through with you."

}
choice("Kill the noble and be done with it.", Ch11a-Thousands, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Thousands, v_ch11_kill_noble = 1)
choice("Tell him to get out of here and to leave the slave with us.", Ch11a-Thousands, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Thousands, v_ch11_kill_noble = 0)


ID: Ch11a-Thousands
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_kill_noble == 1) {
 Without a word, I unhook the crossbow from my pack, crank the winch, and seat a bolt in the groove.

"What is that supposed to be?" the nobleman says, scrambling to his feet. "A threat? You won't shoot, boy. You haven't the stomach for it. You're an insolent peasant, nothing more. Shall I tell you how we handle peasants in Varathia?"

"Not really," I say, and put a bolt straight between his eyes.

The iron head punches through bone with a wet crack. The nobleman drops like an empty coat, dead before his knees hit the cobbles. The street goes dead quiet, onlookers staring in petrified silence at the corpse. As I slide the bow back into its harness, slow, measured clapping echoes behind me.

"Marvelous!" Eiden says, drifting lazily above the cobbles. "My sincerest congratulations, Barry, on a clean kill."

His smile sharpens.

"Now then. If you don't mind my asking: what is your plan for the other thousands of slaves in this city?"

"Excuse me?" I say.

"Surely you realize she isn't the only slave in Varathia," Eiden says. "There are tens of thousands just like her, enduring hell this very second, unsure if they'll see nightfall. Are you going to save them all, Barry?"

His smile stretches wider.

"Unless, of course, you only rescue the ones convenient enough to cross your path—so you can pat yourself on the back and pretend you made a difference."

}
#if(v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 1) {
 "Listen carefully, because I'm only saying this once," I say. "Get out of my sight, and leave the girl where she is. Understood?"

"The slave?!" the nobleman spits. "You forfeit your life and the lives of your companions for worthless property? Take her, outsider. But don't think for a second you're getting her in one piece."

He whips a knife from his coat and lunges for her throat. Before the blade connects, I catch his wrist with both hands, wrenching it backward until bone pops. He screeches, the knife tumbling into the dirt.

"You'll burn for this, outsider!" the nobleman roars, stumbling back clutching his broken wrist. "All of you! Including you, herbalist!"

Rose pales as he jabs a shaking finger in her direction.

"That's right! I know your face! I know your house! Say goodbye to your quiet little life. When my guard arrives, I will make your life a living—"

A column of white-hot fire erupts around him.

Within two seconds, the nobleman is reduced to a mound of smoking ash. The flame vanishes as cleanly as it appeared. The alley goes silent, onlookers staring in sheer terror at the scorched cobbles.

}
#if(v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 2) {
 "Listen carefully, because I'm only saying this once," I say. "Get out of my sight, and leave the girl where she is. Understood?"

"The slave?!" the nobleman spits. "You forfeit your life and the lives of your companions for worthless property? Take her, outsider. But don't think for a second you're getting her in one piece."

He lunges to snatch his fallen knife from the mud, but I bring my heavy boot down across his knuckles with a sickening crunch. He howls, yanking his mangled hand back.

"You'll burn for this, outsider!" the nobleman roars, stumbling back clutching his hand. "All of you! Including you, herbalist!"

Rose pales as he points a finger at her.

"That's right! I know your face! I know your house! Say goodbye to your quiet little life. When my guard arrives, I will make your life a living—"

A column of white-hot fire erupts around him.

Within two seconds, he is ash. The flame vanishes. The crowd stares in petrified silence at the grey mound on the stones.

}
#if(v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 1 || v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 2) {
 "See, Barry? That is how you finish a job properly," Eiden says, hands tucked into his sleeves. "You wouldn't want to bring ruin down on your gracious hostess, would you?"

"I suppose not..." I mutter.

Eiden smiles.

"Now then. If you don't mind my asking: what is your plan for the other thousands of slaves in this city?"

"Excuse me?" I say.

"Surely you realize she isn't the only slave in Varathia," Eiden says. "There are tens of thousands just like her, enduring hell this very second, unsure if they'll see nightfall. Are you going to save them all, Barry?"

His smile widens.

"Unless, of course, you only rescue the ones convenient enough to cross your path—so you can pat yourself on the back and pretend you made a difference."

}
choice(""Yes, I'm going to save them all."", Ch11a-Difference, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Difference, v_ch11_difference = 1)
choice(""Do I look like a mage to you? Why don't you save them all if you care so much?"", Ch11a-Difference, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Difference, v_ch11_difference = 2)
choice(""Should I just ignore everyone who needs help because I can't save the whole world?"", Ch11a-Difference, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Difference, v_ch11_difference = 3)


ID: Ch11a-Difference
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_difference == 1) {
 "Yes, I'm going to save them all," I say. "I'm going to do what you refuse to do, with all your godlike power. I'm going to do it even though I'm not a sage, or a stillwater, or whatever supreme being you think you are. I have no idea how, but I will find a way—and when I do, I'll come find you and shove it down your throat. Does that answer your question?"

As I speak, Eiden's eyes open for the first time since I met him. Not with wrath, but with a sudden, piercing shock that softens into deep sorrow and regret.

When I finish, his eyelids drift closed once more, and a faint smile touches his lips.

"It does indeed," he says softly. "I look forward to seeing your words tested. But for now, my part here is concluded."

He rises into the air, preparing to depart.

"One last detail before I leave," he calls down to Daren and Kate. "The enchantment I laid upon Barry was an area spell. It touched everyone in my presence. The two of you possess the exact same gift as your friend. The effect is permanent. Do with it what you will. Farewell!"

}
#if(v_ch11_difference == 2) {
 "Do I look like a mage to you? Why don't you save them all if you care so much?"

As the question lands, Eiden slowly opens his eyes. I brace for fury, but what meets me is a quiet, aching sorrow.

He closes them again, a wistful smile returning to his face.

"Perhaps I will, Barry," he murmurs. "Perhaps I will. In any case, my work here is finished."

He drifts into the air.

"One last detail before I leave," he calls down to Daren and Kate. "The enchantment I laid upon Barry was an area spell. It touched everyone in my presence. The two of you possess the exact same gift as your friend. The effect is permanent. Do with it what you will. Farewell!"

}
#if(v_ch11_difference == 3) {
 "Should I just ignore everyone who needs help because I can't save the whole world?"

Eiden tilts his head, considering the thought with a faint hum.

"Put that way, I suppose not!" he says lightly. "Very well! My work here is done."

He rises into the air.

"One last detail before I leave," he calls down to Daren and Kate. "The enchantment I laid upon Barry was an area spell. It touched everyone in my presence. The two of you possess the exact same gift as your friend. The effect is permanent. Do with it what you will. Farewell!"

}
 He snaps his fingers—and vanishes without a trace.

"Sir..." A small, hesitant tug at my sleeve pulls my attention down. "Excuse me, sir..."

I turn to find the girl dropping into a deep, stiff bow, her forehead nearly touching her knees.

"My name is Ella," she says, her voice barely above a whisper. "Though most called me only 'slave girl.' I know I am not much, but if you will have me, I swear to serve you faithfully until the day I die. Give me my first command, and our pact is sealed."

choice(""Sure thing! I guess you can start by shining my boots! They're a little dirty."", Ch11a-Boots, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Boots)
choice(""Wait, I didn't mean to become your new master. I only meant to set you free!"", Ch11a-Free, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Free)
choice(""Uh, okay. How about coming with us to Rose's house, for the time being?"", Ch11a-House, v_current_scene = Ch11a-House)


ID: Ch11a-Boots
TEXT:

 "Sure thing! I guess you can start by shining my boots! They're a little dirty."

"As you command, master," Ella says, dropping to her knees in the mud and lifting her ragged sleeve toward my boot.

"Wait, hold on!" I jump backward. "It was a joke! A joke!"

A sharp, vicious tug on the crown of my head wrenches my neck backward as Kate yanks a fistful of my hair.

"You and your mouth," Kate growls into my ear. "Couldn't you rein it in for thirty seconds?"

"Master?" the girl asks, looking up from the cobblestones in sheer bewilderment.

"Ella, you don't have to do anything for me," I say, rubbing the sore spot on my scalp as Kate lets go. "You can go wherever you want. Do whatever you like."

"I... I don't understand..." Ella whispers, trembling.

"Barry means you are welcome to come with us," Rose says gently, placing a hand on the girl's shoulder. "My house is only a short walk from here."

"Y-yes, sir," Ella stammers. "Of course."

"First, let's see to those cuts and bruises," Rose says. "That tall gentleman in the armor is our healer. He'll have you patched up in no time. Won't you, Daren?"

Daren stands staring down the alley, one hand resting heavy on his sword pommel. It takes him several heartbeats to hear her.

"Right," he mutters, pulling himself back. "Yes. Come here. Let me see."

While Daren kneels by the girl, Rose pulls Kate and me aside, keeping her voice low.

"Barry, you cannot rescue a girl from a butcher and tell her she's free to wander off," Rose whispers. "That is almost as cruel as leaving her in the gutter."

"What do you mean?" I ask.

Rose sighs, glancing over her shoulder.

"Even if she understands what freedom means, where does she go? No coin, no family, no roof, marked as a slave by everyone in this district. If she finds work at all, the first gang of slavers who spots her will drag her off in chains, and no one will lift a finger to stop them. You give her a single afternoon of hope before the city swallows her again."

"If you're explaining all this, I take it you have a better idea?" I ask.

"I'm hiring her as a maid at my estate," Rose says, a small smile returning. "I worry constantly about leaving Suzie and Kevin whenever I'm away on herb runs. I used to ask Enrique to look in on them, but having someone living in the house would put my mind at ease."

"Are you certain it's wise to leave your little brother and sister with someone you pulled from the gutter ten minutes ago?" Kate asks quietly.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Details, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Details)


ID: Ch11a-Free
TEXT:

 "Wait, I didn't mean to become your new master. I only meant to set you free!"

Ella's eyes widen in sheer horror, tears welling instantly. "Y-you're... going to kill me?"

"What?!" I wave my hands. "No! No, you misunderstood—"

"Barry means you are welcome to come with us," Rose steps in gently, laying a steady hand on the girl's shoulder. "My home is only a short walk from here."

"Y-yes, sir," Ella stammers. "Of course."

"First, let's see to those cuts and bruises," Rose says. "That tall gentleman in the armor is our healer. He'll have you patched up in no time. Won't you, Daren?"

Daren stands staring down the alley, one hand resting heavy on his sword pommel. It takes him several heartbeats to hear her.

"Right," he mutters, pulling himself back. "Yes. Come here. Let me see."

While Daren kneels by the girl, Rose pulls Kate and me aside, keeping her voice low.

"Barry, you cannot tell a slave she is free," Rose whispers.

"Why not?" I ask.

"Because slaves are never freed," Rose says flatly. "Masters only say those words when a worker is broken beyond repair, right before they cut their throat in the alley."

"She'll understand once I explain it," I say.

Rose sighs, glancing over her shoulder.

"Even if she understands what freedom means, where does she go? No coin, no family, no roof, marked as a slave by everyone in this district. If she finds work at all, the first gang of slavers who spots her will drag her off in chains, and no one will lift a finger to stop them. You give her a single afternoon of hope before the city swallows her again."

"If you're explaining all this, I take it you have a better idea?" I ask.

"I'm hiring her as a maid at my estate," Rose says, a small smile returning. "I worry constantly about leaving Suzie and Kevin whenever I'm away on herb runs. I used to ask Enrique to look in on them, but having someone living in the house would put my mind at ease."

"Are you certain it's wise to leave your little brother and sister with someone you pulled from the gutter ten minutes ago?" Kate asks quietly.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Details, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Details)


ID: Ch11a-House
TEXT:

 "Uh, okay. How about coming with us to Rose's house, for the time being?"

"As you command, master," Ella says, eyes fixed on the mud.

"Barry!" Kate scolds under her breath. "You cannot invite strangers into Rose's home without asking. She's already taking in the rest of us."

"It's all right, Kate," Rose says, offering a warm smile. "I was about to suggest the very same thing. But first, let's see to those wounds."

She turns toward the girl.

"That tall gentleman in the armor is our healer. He'll have you patched up in no time. Won't you, Daren?"

Daren stands staring down the alley, one hand resting heavy on his sword pommel. It takes him several heartbeats to hear her.

"Right," he mutters, pulling himself back. "Yes. Come here. Let me see."

While Daren kneels by the girl, Rose pulls Kate and me aside, keeping her voice low.

"Listen to me," Rose whispers. "No one is to mention the word 'freedom' to that girl. I'm looking at you, Barry."

"What's wrong with letting her know she's free to choose?" I ask.

Rose sighs, glancing over her shoulder.

"Even if she understands what freedom means, where does she go? No coin, no family, no roof, marked as a slave by everyone in this district. If she finds work at all, the first gang of slavers who spots her will drag her off in chains, and no one will lift a finger to stop them. You give her a single afternoon of hope before the city swallows her again."

"If you're explaining all this, I take it you have a better idea?" I ask.

"I'm hiring her as a maid at my estate," Rose says, a small smile returning. "I worry constantly about leaving Suzie and Kevin whenever I'm away on herb runs. I used to ask Enrique to look in on them, but having someone living in the house would put my mind at ease."

"Are you certain it's wise to leave your little brother and sister with someone you pulled from the gutter ten minutes ago?" Kate asks quietly.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Details, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Details)


ID: Ch11a-Details
TEXT:

 "Given how thoroughly slaves are broken to obey, I doubt Ella would ever raise a hand against the children," Rose says. "Besides, I won't be traveling again for weeks. I'll have ample time to learn her character."

She pauses, looking down at her own hands.

"Besides... Daren and Eiden were right. I haven't been honest with myself. I looked away because looking was easier. I have no excuse. I cannot overturn the laws of Varathia, but I can help the person standing right in front of me. Watching Barry step in gave me the courage to do something. And right now, offering this girl a roof and an honest living is what I can do."

"All done," Daren announces, rising from the cobbles as the white glow fades from his hands.

"Thank you, sir," Ella whispers.

Without the swelling and dried blood, Ella's features emerge: high cheekbones, large brown eyes, full lips, and a delicate jawline. Beneath the grime and tattered sacking is someone who would turn heads in any town square. If she had been born to an ordinary family, she would have had suitors lining up at her door every morning. If she had lived an ordinary life...

"We shouldn't linger," Rose says, glancing at the silent crowd watching from the alleys. "Let's collect Hadrik and Flower, then take the side streets to the estate."

Hadrik steps out of the armor shop as we approach, looking disgruntled. "Why the rush? I thought we were waiting for Flower."

"Things happened," Rose says. "We'll explain when we reach the house."

"Uh-huh," Hadrik grunts, his eyes dropping to Ella. "And I take it those things include recruiting a new companion while my back was turned?"

Rose smiles playfully. "You catch on quickly."

"Name's Hadrik," he says, extending a thick, calloused palm toward the girl. "Friends call me the dwarf. Pleased to meet you, lass!"

Ella flinches violently, shrinking back from his massive hand. She looks pleadingly at Rose and me; when I give her a nod, she hesitantly reaches out and touches his fingers with a limp, trembling grip.

"Never met a dwarf before, have you?" Hadrik asks with an easy grin.

"It's complicated," Rose says. "House first. Come on, let's find Flower."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Modest, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Modest)


ID: Ch11a-Modest
TEXT:

 We find Flower a few streets back, standing on the corner where we first parted. She has already fastened the fresh brass chain to Arraka's amulet. While she looks surprised that we abandoned our original meeting spot, she keeps her questions to herself when Rose gestures for silence.

We take a winding path through the back alleys to shake off any onlookers from the market. As we move deeper into the city, leaning timber shacks give way to paved roads, tall stone walls, and iron gates. The stench of open drains fades into clipped boxwood and flowering vines. When Rose finally unlatches a pair of grand wrought-iron gates, we find ourselves standing before a sprawling two-story limestone mansion set within grounds the size of a town commons.

"Kate, you can relax your aura suppression now," Rose says as we follow the gravel drive. "My siblings have never heard of banshees. There's no need to hide from them."

"Rose, not to pry," Hadrik says, looking around at the manicured hedges, "but didn't you mention you and your siblings lived a modest life in town?"

Rose flushes.

"I didn't purchase this estate," she says. "It belonged to my ex-husband before our divorce. Enrique owned several manors in the upper districts and insisted I keep this one. I intended to refuse, but with two young children and my bedridden father to care for, our old two-room cottage was simply unmanageable."

"Ah, trying to win you back with a parting gift, was he?" Hadrik asks with a grin.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Passion, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Passion)


ID: Ch11a-Passion
TEXT:

 "Not quite," Rose says. "Enrique was never a man who could belong to one person. After the papers were signed, he told me marriage made him feel like a caged hawk denied the open sky, longing to soar in search of radiant stars. Then he added that no matter how many stars he visited, I would always remain the sun outshining them all, and that he would never love another woman with such devotion."

Rose giggles softly.

"It was always impossible to keep a straight face when he started on about poetry," she says with a smile.

"Rose! Rose is back!" a high voice shouts across the lawn. "Kevin, come quick!"

An eight-year-old girl comes tearing around the side of the house, waving madly to a nine-year-old boy hot on her heels.

"Rose, look!" the girl calls out, holding up a small plastic casing with both hands. "Billy is fourteen today! And he's still running around, see?"

"Wonderful work, Suzie!" Rose beams, crouching down to pat her sister's head. "I was a little worried while I was out in the woods, but I see Billy was in good hands."

I glance at the trinket in Suzie's palm. The thick casing, the monochrome glass display, the tiny buttons—it looks uncannily like my stat device. Far more than I realized. Could it be another lessathi relic passed off as a child's diversion?

choice("Ask Suzie if I can borrow her toy for a second to take a look at it.", Ch11a-Toy, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Toy, v_ch11_toy = 1)
choice("Leave Suzie alone.", Ch11a-Toy, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Toy, v_ch11_toy = 0)


ID: Ch11a-Toy
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch11_symbol,1) if (v_ch11_toy == 1)
achievement("A mysterious symbol",v_ac_ch11_symbol)
#if(v_ch11_toy == 1) {
 "Suzie," I say, crouching beside her. "Can I see that toy for a second? Just want to check something."

"No!" She snaps it behind her back. "It's mine!"

Hardly a surprising response.

"How about a trade?" I pull my stat booster from my belt. "You hold this while I take a look at yours. Deal?"

She tries to look unimpressed, but her eyes latch onto the glass screen immediately. She hands over her trinket in exchange.

I turn the casing over in my fingers. Along the outer seam sits a tiny stamped glyph—three prongs curved outward like a bent trident. Beyond that, nothing: no aura hum, no faint static buzzing against my fingertips. Just a simple glass screen and tin circuits.

"Wise to hand that over, Barry?" Hadrik asks with a chuckle. "What if she scrambles your combat stats?"

"Interface is locked," I say. "All she can do is stare at the glass."

I hand the toy back and retrieve my device.

Suzie pouts, thrusting the booster back into my palm. "You tricked me! It doesn't do anything. That's not fair!"

"Life isn't fair, kid," I tell her, slipping the device back into my pocket. "Good lesson to learn early."

"Don't listen to him, Suzie," Rose laughs. "Barry is only grumpy because his parents gave him broken toys."

Suzie giggles. "Poor Barry!"

"Poor Barry!" Kevin mimics, bounding around her.

"Rose," Daren cuts in, his voice flat and worn. "Could you show me to a room? I'd like to lie down."

"Of course," Rose says softly. "Follow me."

"We're coming too!" the siblings chime in together.

Rose turns to the rest of us. "Please, make yourselves comfortable. You're welcome to wait in the main parlor."

"We're fine out here," Hadrik says. "We'll take the garden tables."

Rose nods and leads Daren and the children inside.

}
#if(v_ch11_toy == 0) {
 I leave the girl to her game. The resemblance is almost certainly a coincidence.

"That shield is huge!" Kevin exclaims, skidding to a halt in front of Daren. "I have a lead soldier with one just like it! Can I touch it? Can I?"

"Another time, lad," Daren says quietly.

He turns toward Rose.

"Rose. Could you show me to a room? I'd like to lie down."

"Of course," Rose says softly. "Follow me."

"We're coming too!" the children chorus.

Rose glances back at us. "Please, make yourselves comfortable. You're welcome to wait in the main parlor."

"We're fine out here," Hadrik says. "We'll take the garden tables."

Rose nods and leads Daren and the siblings through the double doors.

}
choice("Continue", Ch11a-Prevented, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Prevented) if (v_ch11_saved_slave == 3)
choice("Continue", Ch11a-Task, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Task) if (v_ch11_saved_slave != 3)


ID: Ch11a-Prevented
TEXT:

 "Now that we're off our feet," Hadrik says, settling onto a wooden garden bench, "will someone explain what happened to Daren that turned him even more sour than usual?"

Kate and I exchange a grim look. Between the two of us, we give Hadrik and Flower a full accounting of the alley, the noble, and the slaughter.

"Damn it!" Hadrik growls, slamming a fist into his palm. "If only I hadn't wasted time browsing iron. If I'd been standing there, I would have taken the power in Daren's place and broken that swine in half."

He pauses, his dark eyes fixing on Kate and me.

"Still, I don't get it. Both of you were standing right there. You could have stopped it. Why didn't you do anything?"

choice(""It was none of our business."", Ch11a-Business, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Business)
choice(""It would have been too risky."", Ch11a-Risky, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Risky)


ID: Ch11a-Business
TEXT:

 "It was none of our business."

"None of our business?!" Hadrik's voice thunders across the lawn, fury burning in his eyes. "You watched an innocent girl butchered in the dirt and called it none of your business?! Is that what you're telling me?!"

"Hadrik," Kate says, her voice flat. "We can't save everyone..."

"That doesn't mean we step over corpses when they fall at our feet!" Hadrik slams both fists onto the timber table, shaking the planks. "I barely recognize either of you! It's like you've both turned into Arraka!"

"And what's so terrible about that?" Arraka's voice rasps from the amulet.

Hadrik snatches his pack from the grass and storms across the lawn toward the mansion doors.

"Can you believe this fellow?" Arraka asks with a cackle. "One minute he's all brawling and ale, the next he's weeping like the holy healer! What's the fuss over a dead slave anyway?"

"Be quiet, Arraka," Flower snaps.

"What?" Arraka sneers. "The wretch was slacking off and talking back to her owner. I would have gutted her too. Probably would have done it just for the laugh!"

"Arraka. Do you want your amulet sealed for another twenty years?"

The metal goes dead silent. Arraka clearly has no appetite for another two decades in the dark.

"Did something happen to Hadrik?" Rose asks, coming down the portico steps. "He asked for his room and marched right past me without a word. He looked furious."

"He didn't take the news about the slave girl well," Kate says quietly.

"Oh..." Rose looks down. "I see."

"Could you show us our rooms too?" I ask, eager to break the tension. "I could use an actual bed."

"Of course," Rose says. "Follow me."

Rose settles us in, then departs for the Sparrow's district with her wicker basket of remedies, leaving the estate in our hands until sundown. Nobody is in the mood for company; we spend the afternoon in our separate quarters. By dusk Rose returns, and Hadrik rejoins us in the courtyard, somewhat cooled down. Daren's door stays firmly shut.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Clown, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Clown, v_available_points = +2, v_available_points_aux = +2)


ID: Ch11a-Risky
TEXT:

 "It would have been too risky."

"Too risky?!" Hadrik's voice thunders across the lawn. "Are you serious right now?! You think crossing local lords is more dangerous than storming an ogre stronghold?!"

"Hadrik," Kate says, her voice flat. "We can't save everyone..."

"That doesn't mean we step over corpses when they fall at our feet!" Hadrik slams both fists onto the timber table, shaking the planks. "I barely recognize either of you! It's like you've both turned into Arraka!"

"And what's so terrible about that?" Arraka's voice rasps from the amulet.

Hadrik snatches his pack from the grass and storms across the lawn toward the mansion doors.

"Can you believe this fellow?" Arraka asks with a cackle. "One minute he's all brawling and ale, the next he's weeping like the holy healer! What's the fuss over a dead slave anyway?"

"Be quiet, Arraka," Flower snaps.

"What?" Arraka sneers. "The wretch was slacking off and talking back to her owner. I would have gutted her too. Probably would have done it just for the laugh!"

"Arraka. Do you want your amulet sealed for another twenty years?"

The metal goes dead silent. Arraka clearly has no appetite for another two decades in the dark.

"Did something happen to Hadrik?" Rose asks, coming down the portico steps. "He asked for his room and marched right past me without a word. He looked furious."

"He didn't take the news about the slave girl well," Kate says quietly.

"Oh..." Rose looks down. "I see."

"Could you show us our rooms too?" I ask, eager to break the tension. "I could use an actual bed."

"Of course," Rose says. "Follow me."

Rose settles us in, then departs for the Sparrow's district with her wicker basket of remedies, leaving the estate in our hands until sundown. Nobody is in the mood for company; we spend the afternoon in our separate quarters. By dusk Rose returns, and Hadrik rejoins us in the courtyard, somewhat cooled down. Daren's door stays firmly shut.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Clown, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Clown, v_available_points = +2, v_available_points_aux = +2)


ID: Ch11a-Clown
TEXT:

 "Sorry about earlier," Hadrik tells Kate and me as we sit at the garden table in the evening light. "I lost my temper. I wasn't there, so I had no right to throw stones. I know you both did what you thought best under the circumstances."

"No need to apologize," Kate says quietly. "What happened shook all of us. I don't blame you for being angry."

Rose's siblings dart past the tables every few minutes, racing between the hedges.

"Hey," Kevin says, skidding to a halt in front of Flower. "Why are you dressed like a clown?"

Flower bristles, her eyes narrowing. "I'm not dressed like a clown! Are you blind? These are the acrobat silks I wore on the high trapeze back at the circus!"

"Trapeze, acrobats, clowns—same thing," Kevin says with a shrug.

Flower looks ready to summon a fireball right between his eyes.

"Don't mind him," Suzie tells Flower, stepping between them. "Kevin is just being obnoxious. Your clothes are fine. And your hair is gorgeous."

Flower blinks, thrown completely off balance. "Uh... thanks, I guess."

"I wish I had long red hair like yours," Suzie says. "Will you let me braid it? Please?"

"No!" Flower gasps, clutching her waist-length locks with both hands as if defending them from a griffin.

"Okay," Suzie says with a crestfallen pout.

Night settles over the estate. We disperse to our rooms after agreeing to meet at first light to plan our next move. I take full advantage of a hot bath in the tiled washroom—a glorious luxury after days of freezing mountain runoff. Back in my quarters, my stat booster emits a series of sharp chimes. New points available. With tournament threats escalating, I need to allocate them carefully.

I pull the device from my belt and study the glowing glyphs on the screen.

choice("Invest points now", Ch11a-Stats-spent1, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Stats-spent1, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", Ch11a-Serious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Serious)


ID: Ch11a-Stats-spent1
TEXT:

 "Sorry about earlier," Hadrik tells Kate and me as we sit at the garden table in the evening light. "I lost my temper. I wasn't there, so I had no right to throw stones. I know you both did what you thought best under the circumstances."

"No need to apologize," Kate says quietly. "What happened shook all of us. I don't blame you for being angry."

Rose's siblings dart past the tables every few minutes, racing between the hedges.

"Hey," Kevin says, skidding to a halt in front of Flower. "Why are you dressed like a clown?"

Flower bristles, her eyes narrowing. "I'm not dressed like a clown! Are you blind? These are the acrobat silks I wore on the high trapeze back at the circus!"

"Trapeze, acrobats, clowns—same thing," Kevin says with a shrug.

Flower looks ready to summon a fireball right between his eyes.

"Don't mind him," Suzie tells Flower, stepping between them. "Kevin is just being obnoxious. Your clothes are fine. And your hair is gorgeous."

Flower blinks, thrown completely off balance. "Uh... thanks, I guess."

"I wish I had long red hair like yours," Suzie says. "Will you let me braid it? Please?"

"No!" Flower gasps, clutching her waist-length locks with both hands as if defending them from a griffin.

"Okay," Suzie says with a crestfallen pout.

Night settles over the estate. We disperse to our rooms after agreeing to meet at first light to plan our next move. I take full advantage of a hot bath in the tiled washroom—a glorious luxury after days of freezing mountain runoff. Back in my quarters, my stat booster emits a series of sharp chimes. New points available. With tournament threats escalating, I need to allocate them carefully.

I pull the device from my belt and study the glowing glyphs on the screen.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Serious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Serious)


ID: Ch11a-Serious
TEXT:

 Once I finish configuring the stat booster, I slide it back into my pocket and stretch out on the mattress. Sleep comes fast.

A heavy fist pounding against the wooden door jars me awake hours later.

"Barry! Still asleep?" Daren's voice booms through the panel. "Up and dressed. We're waiting in the yard. Hurry up or we leave you behind. I mean it."

Heavy footsteps tramp down the staircase. Through the window, the morning sun sits barely an hour above the rooftops. In the yard below, the others are already gathered, Rose's siblings tearing laps around the lawn.

I roll out of bed, pull on my clothes, and leave the heavy pack behind—assuming this is just a morning supply run. I slide my dagger into my belt, pocket some silver, and head downstairs.

"Where are we headed?" I ask Rose as I join the group on the gravel drive.

"A clinic in the Beggar's district," Rose says. "I stopped by yesterday after visiting Sparrow's and found dozens of wounded without proper care. None in critical danger, but Daren's healing spells will work wonders. I only asked Daren, but Hadrik insisted on the whole group."

"Safer together," Hadrik grunts. "Just because there's paving stones underfoot doesn't mean we let our guard down. We're still in the middle of a deadly tournament."

"If everyone is ready, let's move," Rose says. "We'll take the direct avenue this morning, so keep close."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Incriminated, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Incriminated)


ID: Ch11a-Task
TEXT:

 "I can't believe this!" Hadrik grumbles, crossing his thick arms. "So Flower and I are the only ones left who can't lay a finger on the locals because we weren't standing in the alley when Eiden cast his spell?"

"I never had that problem," Flower says. "I'm not enrolled in the tournament, remember? The ward doesn't touch me."

"Great!" Hadrik says. "Just grand! Why did I have to browse iron? They didn't even have a decent axe!"

Beside me, Ella sits stiff as an oak post, her fingers digging into the hem of her burlap tunic.

"Excuse me, master," she whispers, keeping her eyes glued to the grass. "I... I don't mean to intrude, but you haven't assigned me a task since we arrived."

"Don't worry about it," I tell her. "Just sit and rest."

"Y-yes, sir..." she murmurs.

Instead of soothing her, the words seem to ratchet her panic higher. She sits rigid, biting her lower lip, sweat beading along her hairline as she stares at the wood grain of the table.

"There you are!" Rose calls out, coming down the portico steps and taking a seat beside Flower. "Daren is settled in his room. Shall I show the rest of you upstairs?"

"Later," Hadrik says. "Ella's been chewing her knuckles raw for twenty minutes because she has no chore. Don't you have some yard work that needs tending?"

"Oh, I couldn't possibly set her to work after what she endured today!" Rose turns to the girl with a warm smile. "Ella, go lie down for as long as you need. Once you're rested, I'll give you a proper tour of the estate. All right?"

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Loyalty, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Loyalty)


ID: Ch11a-Loyalty
TEXT:

 Ella shoots to her feet, knocking her knees against the bench, her face pale with terror.

"There is no need for the test!" she cries out, voice cracking. "I will work! I won't disappoint you! I swear it!"

The sudden outburst shocks the table into silence.

"Ella, no one is testing you," Rose says gently, holding up a calming hand. "Please, sit down. You're exhausted."

"Yes, sir..." Ella whispers, trembling so hard her teeth chatter as she lowers herself back onto the bench.

"Rose, what is she talking about?" I ask. "What test?"

"I... have no idea," Rose says.

"She's talking about a test of loyalty," Kate says, her voice flat and steady. "A standard procedure to break a captive's will so they never attempt escape. They used it constantly at the Beacon."

Hadrik knits his brow. "Why would she think we're testing her? None of us mentioned anything of the sort."

"Because you're being kind," Kate says. "That is how every loyalty test begins. At the Beacon, a handler or an unfamiliar guard would act gentle out of nowhere. Once the child let their guard down, the guard would whisper an offer to help them escape. If the orphan believed them and ran, the same guard dragged them back into the courtyard, announced they had failed the loyalty test, and beat them half to death in front of the others—or hung them from the gate as a lesson. After that, even if genuine rescue arrived, no child dared take a hand. Fear kept them in their cells better than iron bars."

Rose presses a hand against her chest, horrified. "Gods above... That is monstrous. But if that's what she expects, how do we convince her we mean no harm?"

"You don't," Kate says bluntly. "Words won't reach her. She assumes this conversation is part of the act to make her let her guard down. She believes that if she accepts rest, you'll declare her useless and cut her throat, exactly like that noble."

choice("Ask Kate if there's anything we can do to make the girl less suspicious of us.", Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Suspicious)
choice("Order Ella to go trim the bushes from the garden, in order to ease her fears.", Ch11a-Bushes, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Bushes)


ID: Ch11a-Suspicious
TEXT:

 "Is there anything we can do to make Ella less suspicious of us?" I ask Kate.

"Stop pampering her," Kate says. "It only deepens her panic."

Rose's jaw sets with quiet determination. She turns to Ella, her voice firm and clear.

"Ella. You will march into that house and bring me a glass of fresh water. The kitchen is off the main hall on the left. There is a hand pump by the window, and clean glasses on the upper shelf. Understood?"

Ella leaps to her feet, bowing deeply. "Yes, sir! Right away!"

She bolts toward the double doors. Rose lets out a long breath, slumping back against the bench.

"I hope that wasn't too harsh..." Rose murmurs.

"Giving her orders is a temporary bridge," Kate says. "We need a way to integrate her without reinforcing the cage."

Ella re-emerges from the mansion carrying a full tumbler, with Suzie and Kevin skipping circles around her feet.

Rose's eyes light up as she watches them approach. "I think I have an idea."

"You're really pretty!" Suzie announces, trotting alongside Ella.

Ella glances down, flustered. "Thank you, miss—sir."

Suzie giggles. "You're funny!"

Rose accepts the glass with a nod. "Ella, I have your assignment for the rest of the day."

Ella straightens, hands pressed to her sides. "What would you have me do, sir?"

"Follow my little sister Suzie wherever she goes," Rose says, "and obey her instructions until dusk."

Ella nods solemnly. "Of course. I will not fail."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Braided, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Braided)


ID: Ch11a-Bushes
TEXT:

 "Fine," I say. "Ella, go trim those hedges along the gravel drive. That's an order."

Ella scrambles up immediately, bowing low. "Yes, sir!"

She hurries off toward the tool shed.

"Damn it, Barry!" Hadrik mutters. "We're trying to help the girl, not run her into the ground!"

"No, this is better," Kate says. "An assigned task gives her predictable rules to hold onto while her nerves settle. But we need a lasting solution."

Ella begins trimming the boxwood with heavy shears. Suzie and Kevin immediately run across the lawn to investigate.

Rose watches the three of them, a smile forming. "I think I have an idea."

"You're really pretty!" Suzie tells Ella as the shears click.

Ella pauses, flustered. "Thank you, miss—sir."

Suzie giggles. "You're funny!"

Rose walks over, resting a hand on her sister's shoulder. "Ella, I have a different assignment for you."

Ella lowers the shears, stiffening. "What would you have me do, sir?"

"Follow my little sister Suzie wherever she goes," Rose says, "and obey her instructions until dusk."

Ella nods solemnly. "Of course. I will not fail."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Braided, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Braided)


ID: Ch11a-Braided
TEXT:

 Suzie gasps, bouncing on her heels. "She has to do whatever I say?"

"Whatever you say," Rose promises.

Suzie squeals with delight, catching Ella by the sleeve. "Ella, come on! I know exactly what we're doing first!"

"Yes, miss—sir," Ella says, following the little girl toward the house.

Hadrik rubs his beard. "You certain that's wise?"

"Completely," Rose says. "Come, let me show you to your quarters."

After settling our gear in our rooms, we gather in the gravel drive to see Rose off to the Sparrow's district. Just as we wave goodbye, Suzie bursts through the front doors, towing Ella by the hand.

The transformation is striking. Ella wears a clean emerald dress, her face washed clean of street grit, her dark hair combed and woven into intricate, elegant braids.

"Ta-daaaa!" Suzie beams, hands on her hips.

Hadrik whistles. "Well now. That's quite the change. You do all that braiding, Suzie?"

"Yep!" Suzie says proudly. "Isn't she gorgeous? Turn around, Ella, show them!"

Ella blushes crimson, turning slowly on her heel so we can see the neat plaits. It must have taken an hour of meticulous work.

"Come on, Ella!" Suzie says, catching her wrist again. "Let's play!"

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Exhaustion, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Exhaustion)


ID: Ch11a-Exhaustion
TEXT:

 Hadrik, Kate, Flower, and I spend the rest of the afternoon at the garden tables, sharing pastries Flower conjures from thin air. Daren stays in his room with the latch drawn. Across the lawn, Suzie and Ella play tag and hide-and-seek between the hedges. As the sun dips toward the rooftops, Ella's movements grow heavy and unsteady, her knees buckling slightly with every step.

"Faster!" Suzie laughs, clinging to Ella's shoulders for a piggyback ride. "Faster, Ella!"

Kevin watches them from a bench. "Hey, Suzie. Ella has to do whatever you say, right?"

"Yep!" Suzie calls back.

"Tell her to stand on her hands!"

Suzie skids to a halt, frowning. "What? No! Why would I tell her that?"

"Because it'd look funny!" Kevin says with a grin.

"I'm not ordering her to do that, Kevin!"

"Why not? She has to obey. What's the problem?"

"That doesn't mean we treat her like a circus act!" Suzie snaps.

Kevin rolls his eyes. "You're no fun."

"You're just being mean," Suzie says, "and that's why you have no friends!"

She slides down from Ella's back and wraps her arms tightly around the girl's waist, glaring at her brother. "Don't worry, Ella. I won't let anyone hurt you."

Ella sways on her feet, her face drenched in sweat. "Thank you... miss..."

Her eyes roll back, and she pitches forward onto the turf.

"Ella!" Suzie screams.

Hadrik and I bolt across the grass. Ella is breathing in shallow, rapid gasps, her forehead burning hot against my palm.

"I did this!" Suzie sobs, tears streaming down her cheeks. "I broke her! I shouldn't have asked for the ride! It's all my fault!"

She buries her face in her hands, weeping uncontrollably.

choice("Try to comfort Suzie", Ch11a-Comfort, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Comfort)
choice("Let someone else deal with Suzie and go call for Daren", Ch11a-Someone-else, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Someone-else)


ID: Ch11a-Comfort
TEXT:

 "Suzie, listen to me," I say, resting steady hands on her trembling shoulders. "Ella was going to run until she collapsed no matter what you asked. This is not your fault."

"I'll fetch Daren," Kate says, already sprinting toward the mansion.

"You don't know that!" Suzie cries. "You can't know that!"

"I do know," I tell her firmly. "We tried to make her rest, and she panicked. Rose put her with you because she trusted you to treat her with care. She isn't dying, Suzie. She is simply exhausted. A night of sleep will set her right."

"Barry, help me carry her," Hadrik says, scooping Ella up in his broad arms. "Which room is open?"

"Ground floor, end of the east hall," I say. "Come on, Suzie, stay with us."

We lay Ella on the spare bed. Moments later, Kate and Daren burst into the room.

Daren immediately places his hands over her chest, summoning a pulse of white light. "Why wasn't she resting? She was on the verge of collapse when we brought her in."

"We tried," Kate says. "She believed resting was a failure of obedience that would get her killed."

Daren grinds his teeth, his jaw clenching. "Miserable swine. I'd kill that nobleman twice if I could."

The white radiance sinks into Ella's skin. Her rapid gasping eases into deep, steady breaths.

"The fever is broken," Daren says, lowering his hands. "The exhaustion remains. She needs quiet and rest."

"Gods above, what happened?" Rose asks, stepping through the doorway with her herb basket on her arm.

"Overworked herself until she fainted," Hadrik says.

"I worried that might happen..." Rose steps to the bedside. "Will she be all right?"

"She's fine," Daren says. "Just drained. No more chores today."

Ella's eyelids flutter. Hearing Daren's voice, she struggles to prop herself up on trembling elbows, but her strength gives out and she falls back against the pillows.

"I can... still work..." she whispers, panic flaring in her dark eyes. "Master, please... I can work..."

"Ella, you're awake!" Suzie throws her arms around her neck.

"Please," Ella begs, staring at Rose. "Just give me another order..."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Fever, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Fever, v_available_points = +2, v_available_points_aux = +2)


ID: Ch11a-Someone-else
TEXT:

 "I'll fetch Daren," I say. "Get her to a bed downstairs."

Hadrik scoops Ella up without hesitation. I sprint through the double doors and bound up the stairs, hammering on Daren's door.

"Daren! Open up!"

The latch clicks, and Daren pulls the door wide, his brow furrowed. "Barry? What is it? An ambush?"

"Ella collapsed in the yard. Burning fever and sheer exhaustion. We need your healing."

Daren's face hardens. "Lead the way."

We hurry down to the ground-floor bedroom. Hadrik has already laid Ella on the mattress, with Kate, Suzie, and Flower gathered round.

Daren kneels at once, placing his palms over her chest as white light floods his fingers. "Why wasn't she resting? She was on the verge of collapse when we brought her in."

"We tried," Kate says. "She believed resting was a failure of obedience that would get her killed."

Daren grinds his teeth, his jaw clenching. "Miserable swine. I'd kill that nobleman twice if I could."

The white radiance sinks into Ella's skin. Her rapid gasping eases into deep, steady breaths.

"The fever is broken," Daren says, lowering his hands. "The exhaustion remains. She needs quiet and rest."

"Gods above, what happened?" Rose asks, stepping through the doorway with her herb basket on her arm.

"Overworked herself until she fainted," Hadrik says.

"I worried that might happen..." Rose steps to the bedside. "Will she be all right?"

"She's fine," Daren says. "Just drained. No more chores today."

Ella's eyelids flutter. Hearing Daren's voice, she struggles to prop herself up on trembling elbows, but her strength gives out and she falls back against the pillows.

"I can... still work..." she whispers, panic flaring in her dark eyes. "Master, please... I can work..."

"Ella, you're awake!" Suzie throws her arms around her neck.

"Please," Ella begs, staring at Rose. "Just give me another order..."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Fever, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Fever, v_available_points = +2, v_available_points_aux = +2)


ID: Ch11a-Fever
TEXT:

 "No," Rose says, her voice turning stern as she looks down at the bed. "You cannot work today. You cannot even lift your head. That means as a slave, you are completely useless to me. And the only fate for a useless slave is death."

Ella freezes in sheer terror, too weak to flinch away. Rose takes the girl's trembling hands between her own warm palms, letting the stern mask drop away into a gentle, reassuring smile.

"Except you are not a slave," Rose says softly. "You haven't been a slave since Barry pulled you from that alley. I brought you here to work as a maid. If I truly considered you property, nothing would have stopped me from discarding you the moment you collapsed. You know that better than anyone."

The panic in Ella's eyes slowly gives way to quiet disbelief.

"You are free, Ella," Rose continues. "You are bound by no master and no contract. You may walk out those gates whenever you wish, go wherever you please, and answer to no one. You never have to live in fear again."

Rose pauses, gently squeezing her fingers.

"But if you will grant me a selfish request... I would be deeply grateful if you chose to stay. The coin is modest, and the children will try your patience, but Suzie has already grown attached to you, and we would miss you dearly. I cannot promise wealth, but I can promise a home where you are safe. Will you stay and work with us, Ella?"

Tears spill down Ella's cheeks. "Yes! Of course! I want to stay! Today was the happiest day of my life. I don't want to leave! I swear I will work hard and never betray your trust!"

"Then our agreement is sealed," Rose says with a warm smile, shaking her hand. "Your chores begin once you are fully recovered. For today, your only duty is keeping Suzie company from that bed—she clearly isn't leaving your side anyway."

With Ella's fears put to rest, we leave her in Suzie's care.

Night settles over the estate. We disperse to our rooms after agreeing to meet at first light to plan our next move. I take full advantage of a hot bath in the tiled washroom—a glorious luxury after days of freezing mountain runoff. Back in my quarters, my stat booster emits a series of sharp chimes. New points available. With tournament threats escalating, I need to allocate them carefully.

I pull the device from my belt and study the glowing glyphs on the screen.

choice("Invest points now", Ch11a-Stats-spent2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Stats-spent2, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", Ch11a-Knock, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Knock)


ID: Ch11a-Stats-spent2
TEXT:

 "No," Rose says, her voice turning stern as she looks down at the bed. "You cannot work today. You cannot even lift your head. That means as a slave, you are completely useless to me. And the only fate for a useless slave is death."

Ella freezes in sheer terror, too weak to flinch away. Rose takes the girl's trembling hands between her own warm palms, letting the stern mask drop away into a gentle, reassuring smile.

"Except you are not a slave," Rose says softly. "You haven't been a slave since Barry pulled you from that alley. I brought you here to work as a maid. If I truly considered you property, nothing would have stopped me from discarding you the moment you collapsed. You know that better than anyone."

The panic in Ella's eyes slowly gives way to quiet disbelief.

"You are free, Ella," Rose continues. "You are bound by no master and no contract. You may walk out those gates whenever you wish, go wherever you please, and answer to no one. You never have to live in fear again."

Rose pauses, gently squeezing her fingers.

"But if you will grant me a selfish request... I would be deeply grateful if you chose to stay. The coin is modest, and the children will try your patience, but Suzie has already grown attached to you, and we would miss you dearly. I cannot promise wealth, but I can promise a home where you are safe. Will you stay and work with us, Ella?"

Tears spill down Ella's cheeks. "Yes! Of course! I want to stay! Today was the happiest day of my life. I don't want to leave! I swear I will work hard and never betray your trust!"

"Then our agreement is sealed," Rose says with a warm smile, shaking her hand. "Your chores begin once you are fully recovered. For today, your only duty is keeping Suzie company from that bed—she clearly isn't leaving your side anyway."

With Ella's fears put to rest, we leave her in Suzie's care.

Night settles over the estate. We disperse to our rooms after agreeing to meet at first light to plan our next move. I take full advantage of a hot bath in the tiled washroom—a glorious luxury after days of freezing mountain runoff. Back in my quarters, my stat booster emits a series of sharp chimes. New points available. With tournament threats escalating, I need to allocate them carefully.

I pull the device from my belt and study the glowing glyphs on the screen.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Knock, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Knock)


ID: Ch11a-Knock
TEXT:

 "Knock, knock," Rose says softly from the open doorway. "Am I interrupting?"

"No, just finishing up," I say, sliding the stat booster back into my belt.

"Good. I'm glad you're still awake," she says, stepping inside with a small wooden tray holding a plate of warm muffins. "They won't rival Flower's magic pastries, but I wanted to bring you something for what you did today."

She sets the tray on the bedside table and sits on the edge of the mattress beside me.

"These are great," I tell her after taking a bite. "Though you didn't have to bake on my account. I didn't do anything special. You're the one who thought of Suzie, and you're the one who talked Ella down."

"You know what I mean, Barry," Rose says, her eyes searching mine. "You stood up when everyone else looked away. You put your life on the line for someone you didn't even know."

"No need to thank me," I say. "It felt like the right thing to do. Not looking for a reward."

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Feelings, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Feelings) if (v_ch6_flirt == 2 || v_ch6_flirt == 3 || v_ch6_flirt == 4 || v_ch6_flirt == 6)
choice("Continue", Ch11a-Racing, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Racing) if (v_ch6_flirt == 1 || v_ch6_flirt == 5)


ID: Ch11a-Feelings
TEXT:

#if(v_ch6_flirt == 2) {
 "I know," Rose says with a quiet smile. "And that is why I'm thanking you. Seeing you act gave me the push I needed to stop ignoring what was right in front of me. Watching Ella's face when I told her she could stay was worth more than any coin. After all these years, I finally feel like I'm making a difference. And it started with you."

She laughs softly, ducking her head.

"I can hardly believe you're the same man who made that clumsy remark about my chest the day we met."

"I'm still sorry about that," I say. "I wanted to sound confident, and it came out entirely wrong."

"You said you did it because you liked me..."

Rose pauses, her fingers resting lightly on the quilt.

"Do you... still feel the same way? About me?"

}
#if(v_ch6_flirt != 2) {
 "I know," Rose says with a quiet smile. "And that is why I'm thanking you. Seeing you act gave me the push I needed to stop ignoring what was right in front of me. Watching Ella's face when I told her she could stay was worth more than any coin. After all these years, I finally feel like I'm making a difference. And it started with you."

Rose pauses, her fingers tracing a pattern on the quilt.

"Do you remember what we talked about the first day we met? When I asked if there was something between you and Kate?"

"I remember," I say.

"Do you... still feel the way you did back then? About me?"

}
choice(""Of course not! I like you a lot more now than I did back then!"", Ch11a-Feelings2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Feelings2, v_ch11_romance_rose = 1)
choice(""No, that was just a spur of the moment thing. Don't worry about it!"", Ch11a-Feelings2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Feelings2, v_ch11_romance_rose = 0)


ID: Ch11a-Racing
TEXT:

 "I know," Rose says with a quiet smile. "And that is why I'm thanking you. Seeing you act gave me the push I needed to stop ignoring what was right in front of me. Watching Ella's face when I told her she could stay was worth more than any coin. After all these years, I finally feel like I'm making a difference. And it started with you."

She glances toward the hallway, suddenly hesitant.

"Perhaps we shouldn't be sitting in here like this. I wouldn't want Kate getting the wrong impression if she walked past..."

"Wait, you have the wrong impression," I say. "There's nothing between Kate and me. Honest. My heart didn't jump into my throat when I met her, unlike the afternoon I first saw you."

Rose's cheeks flush warm.

"Your heart raced when you first saw me?" She looks up, her voice softening. "Do you... still feel that way?"

choice(""Of course! I always feel that way when I talk to you."", Ch11a-Racing2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Racing2, v_ch11_romance_rose = 1)
choice(""I'm sorry, I didn't mean it like that. Please, forget what I said!"", Ch11a-Racing2, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Racing2, v_ch11_romance_rose = 0)


ID: Ch11a-Racing2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0) {
 "I'm sorry, I didn't mean it like that. Please, forget what I said!"

"Don't worry," Rose says, her smile returning easily. "Already forgotten."

She stands, picking up a muffin from the tray.

"These really did turn out well. Mind if I take a couple for Suzie? She always claims her baking outdoes mine, and I'd like to dispute the title."

"Take as many as you want," I say. "They're yours."

Rose takes two muffins and pauses by the door.

"Thank you again, Barry. For everything. Good night."

"Good night, Rose."

After she leaves, I eat another muffin and turn in.

Hours later, Daren's voice hauls me out of sleep.

"Barry, wake up! You're not planning on sleeping in again, are you? Get up, or we'll leave you behind!"

Heavy footsteps tramp down the stairs. The morning sun sits barely an hour above the rooftops. In the yard below, the others are already gathered, Rose's siblings tearing laps around the lawn.

I roll out of bed, pull on my clothes, and leave the heavy pack behind—assuming this is just a morning supply run. I slide my dagger into my belt, pocket some silver, and head downstairs.

"About time," Daren grunts as I join the group on the gravel drive. "What took you so long?"

"Where are we going?" I ask Rose, ignoring Daren entirely.

"A clinic in the Beggar's district," Rose says. "I stopped by yesterday after visiting Sparrow's and found dozens of wounded without proper care. None in critical danger, but Daren's healing spells will work wonders. I only asked Daren, but Hadrik insisted on the whole group."

"Safer together," Hadrik grunts. "Just because there's paving stones underfoot doesn't mean we let our guard down. We're still in the middle of a deadly tournament."

"If everyone is ready, let's move," Rose says. "We'll take the direct avenue this morning, so keep close."

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1) {
 "Of course! I always feel that way when I talk to you."

Rose's cheeks flush a deep, warm crimson.

For several heartbeats she sits entirely still, her eyes fixed on mine. Then her chin lifts, and a sudden, quiet resolve settles over her face.

"All right then." She rises from the mattress, crosses to the door, turns the heavy iron key in the lock, and comes back to stand over me.

"What are you—"

She bends down and kisses my mouth, slow and soft, then shifts her weight over me and presses me back into the pillows.

"Wait, wait, wait," I say. "We're doing this now?"

"Why not?" Rose is above me, hair unpinned, and it falls in a curtain around us both. "Would you rather we do it in some ruins, fighting off ogres?"

"Well, no, but — we're still sort of in the middle of a deadly mage tournament."

"We're not getting married, Barry." Her smile is unhurried. "It's one night."

"Yes, but—"

She lays a finger across my lips.

"You talk when you're nervous," she says. "I noticed it the day we met." She leans down until her breath is warm on my mouth. "You don't have to be clever right now. There's no one here to fool."

She kisses me, slower than the first time, and her fingers find the buttons of my shirt and take them one by one, no hurry in her at all. Her hands carry the faint bitter-green smell of the herbs she was grinding this afternoon, and there are small calluses on her fingertips — I feel them on my chest, my shoulders, the base of my throat. Somewhere around the third button I stop composing a joke.

The candle burns low. She laughs once, softly, against my neck — at me or with me, I don't ask. I learn the weight of her, the warmth of her skin under my palms, the way her hair spills across my face when she bends over me, woodsmoke and lavender. At one point she takes my hand and puts it where she wants it, unembarrassed, watching my face while she does. For a long while there is nothing in the world but breath and skin and the small sounds she makes, and the tournament, the dragons, the whole burning continent goes very far away.

Later she lies against my chest, her heartbeat slowing under my hand, one leg thrown across mine. Is this really the same place where I've spent a week fighting for my life? Dragons. Ogres. Treasure stolen out from under sleeping monsters. Honestly, the week is what sounds like the dream. Who in their right mind would believe a story like that?

I pull her closer and close my eyes.

Daren's voice hauls me up out of sleep.

"Barry, wake up! You're not planning on sleeping in again, are you? Get up, or we'll leave you behind!"

He tramps back down the stairs. The sun is barely an hour above the hills. Through the window I can see the others already waiting in the yard, Rose's siblings tearing circles around them, loud and alive.

Her side of the bed is still warm.

Not a dream, then.

}
choice("Continue", Ch11a-Incriminated, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Incriminated) if (v_ch11_romance_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", Ch11a-Romance, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Romance) if (v_ch11_romance_rose == 1)


ID: Ch11a-Feelings2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0) {
 "No, that was just a spur of the moment thing. Don't worry about it!"

"Don't worry," Rose says, her smile returning easily. "Already forgotten."

She stands, picking up a muffin from the tray.

"These really did turn out well. Mind if I take a couple for Suzie? She always claims her baking outdoes mine, and I'd like to dispute the title."

"Take as many as you want," I say. "They're yours."

Rose takes two muffins and pauses by the door.

"Thank you again, Barry. For everything. Good night."

"Good night, Rose."

After she leaves, I eat another muffin and turn in.

Hours later, Daren's voice hauls me out of sleep.

"Barry, wake up! You're not planning on sleeping in again, are you? Get up, or we'll leave you behind!"

Heavy footsteps tramp down the stairs. The morning sun sits barely an hour above the rooftops. In the yard below, the others are already gathered, Rose's siblings tearing laps around the lawn.

I roll out of bed, pull on my clothes, and leave the heavy pack behind—assuming this is just a morning supply run. I slide my dagger into my belt, pocket some silver, and head downstairs.

"About time," Daren grunts as I join the group on the gravel drive. "What took you so long?"

"Where are we going?" I ask Rose, ignoring Daren entirely.

"A clinic in the Beggar's district," Rose says. "I stopped by yesterday after visiting Sparrow's and found dozens of wounded without proper care. None in critical danger, but Daren's healing spells will work wonders. I only asked Daren, but Hadrik insisted on the whole group."

"Safer together," Hadrik grunts. "Just because there's paving stones underfoot doesn't mean we let our guard down. We're still in the middle of a deadly tournament."

"If everyone is ready, let's move," Rose says. "We'll take the direct avenue this morning, so keep close."

}
