choice("Continue", Ch11a-Romance, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Romance) if (v_ch11_romance_rose == 1)


ID: Ch11a-Romance
TEXT:

 I roll out of bed and pull on my clothes, leaving the heavy pack where it sits. Daren likely means a quick run downtown for supplies. I pocket some silver, slide my dagger into my belt, and head downstairs.

Rose isn't in my room when I wake, which means she slipped out at first light to avoid giving the household something to talk about. Probably wise. I don't think I could survive Hadrik's grin if he caught wind of last night.

"About time," Daren grunts as I reach the yard. "What kept you?"

"Where are we headed?" I ask Rose, ignoring Daren entirely.

"A clinic in the Beggar's district," Rose says. "I stopped by yesterday after visiting Sparrow's and found dozens of wounded without proper care. None in critical danger, but Daren's healing spells will work wonders. I only asked Daren, but Hadrik insisted on the whole group."

"Safer together," Hadrik grunts. "Just because there's paving stones underfoot doesn't mean we let our guard down. We're still in the middle of a deadly tournament."

"Is everyone ready?" Rose asks.

"Wait, Rose! I wanted to ask you something!" Flower pipes up.

Rose turns to her. "Yes, Flower? What is it?"

"Did something happen in your room last night?" Flower asks.

"No, nothing. Why do you ask?"

"Well, I thought maybe you were scared of an attack or something, because this morning I saw you come out of Barry's room instead of yours."

Flower's eyes flash bright sapphire as Illuna takes over for half a second, smacking her palm hard against her forehead.

Rose's face turns the color of a ripe beet. Every eye in the courtyard swivels to us.

Kate's mouth opens and shuts, flustered: "Rose... Did you and Barry—"

Hadrik erupts into a booming belly laugh: "Hahaha! Way to go, Barry! Always knew you had it in you!"

Rose waves her hands in frantic denial: "Wait! It's not what you think! It was only one night! We're not—"

Daren clears his throat with a sharp, booming cough that cuts through the chatter like a horn.

"Rose," Daren says loudly, staring straight ahead. "Which way to the clinic? Are we taking the same streets as yesterday?"

"No!" Rose says, hurrying to the head of the group. "No need for the roundabout way. Direct path this morning. Follow me!"

We march through the gates. Not another word is spoken about the night. Daren's intervention was enough to snap everyone back to business. He can be an insufferable moralizer, but when the moment calls for it, the man knows how to clear a room. I owe him one.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Incriminated, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Incriminated)


ID: Ch11a-Incriminated
TEXT:

 We reach the Beggar's district in under an hour. The stench of open drains and rotting refuse hangs heavy over the mud, though after yesterday's visit, the reek hardly registers.

"Rose," Kate says as we turn down a narrow alley, "are you certain it's wise to walk openly with us? After yesterday's incident, we may well be wanted criminals in this city."

"You don't need to worry about me," Rose says. "Years ago, our king passed a law protecting locals from criminal association charges with off-continent mages. Since Varathians rarely associate with outsiders, the law presumes any local traveling in their company is a hostage under duress. King Golmyck has enacted several such protections. He is known as a king of the common folk, and people love him for it."

choice(""Hostages? Has the spell protecting you from outsiders malfunctioned before now?"", Ch11a-Hostages, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Hostages)
choice(""How does this king of the people get along with the nobles?"", Ch11a-People, v_current_scene = Ch11a-People)


ID: Ch11a-Hostages
TEXT:

 "Hostages? Has the spell protecting you from outsiders malfunctioned before now?"

"The ward preventing tournament mages from harming locals has never been deployed before, as far as I know," Rose says. "I suspect the organizers worked with an extraordinary enchanter or obtained an ancient relic. In previous tournaments, off-continent guests were kept under heavy guard and banished at the first sign of trouble. But there were incidents where rogue mages slipped their escorts and caused havoc before they were subdued."

"Did I hear you right?" Hadrik grunts. "Your king is a gnome?"

"Yes," Rose says. "That's right."

"The same gnomes from the north?" Hadrik asks, scratching his beard. "Fragile, beard-less runts smaller than a dwarf? The nomads who wander from province to province every few years?"

"They may lack territory," Rose says, "but no one disputes their brilliance. Gnomes are the finest inventors and engineers of our era. King Golmyck was a celebrated master craftsman before his coronation."

"Elected?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing. "You mean the crown of Thilias isn't hereditary?"

"No," Rose says. "The late monarch died without an heir. On his deathbed, he decreed that his successor be chosen by open ballot, so the realm would have a ruler chosen by the people. The nobility hated the proclamation, but defying a dead king's final wish would have sparked an uprising. Golmyck's inventions and good humor made him the overwhelming favorite, and he won in a landslide."

"I dislike interrupting history lectures," Illuna's voice cuts in, cool and sharp, "but is this market supposed to be completely deserted at mid-morning? Even the merchants haven't unlatched their stalls."

Rose looks around, her brow knitting. "No. You're right. By this hour, the square is usually packed shoulder to shoulder. And the cross streets we passed were empty as well."

Hadrik's hand drops to his axe belt. "Smells like an ambush."

"Definitely an ambush," Arraka's voice rasps from the pendant. "Two casters approaching around that timber corner right now."

A blinding emerald flare floods the alley.

Rose gasps, her eyes rolling back as her knees buckle. She hits the cobblestones before I can reach her. Daren drops beside her in a heartbeat, fingers pressed against her neck, and signals that she has a steady pulse.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Poster, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Poster)


ID: Ch11a-People
TEXT:

 "How does this king of the people get along with the nobles?"

"Terribly," Rose says. "He spent years trying to strip away their aristocratic immunities, which went over about as well as you'd expect. But because he's a political newcomer, the high lords have managed to tie his decrees up in court for the time being."

"Did I hear you right earlier?" Hadrik grunts. "Your king is a gnome?"

"Yes," Rose says. "That's right."

"The same gnomes from the north?" Hadrik asks, scratching his beard. "Fragile, beard-less runts smaller than a dwarf? The nomads who wander from province to province every few years?"

"They may lack territory," Rose says, "but no one disputes their brilliance. Gnomes are the finest inventors and engineers of our era. King Golmyck was a celebrated master craftsman before his coronation."

"Elected?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing. "You mean the crown of Thilias isn't hereditary?"

"No," Rose says. "The late monarch died without an heir. On his deathbed, he decreed that his successor be chosen by open ballot, so the realm would have a ruler chosen by the people. The nobility hated the proclamation, but defying a dead king's final wish would have sparked an uprising. Golmyck's inventions and good humor made him the overwhelming favorite, and he won in a landslide."

"I dislike interrupting history lectures," Illuna's voice cuts in, cool and sharp, "but is this market supposed to be completely deserted at mid-morning? Even the merchants haven't unlatched their stalls."

Rose looks around, her brow knitting. "No. You're right. By this hour, the square is usually packed shoulder to shoulder. And the cross streets we passed were empty as well."

Hadrik's hand drops to his axe belt. "Smells like an ambush."

"Definitely an ambush," Arraka's voice rasps from the pendant. "Two casters approaching around that timber corner right now."

A blinding emerald flare floods the alley.

Rose gasps, her eyes rolling back as her knees buckle. She hits the cobblestones before I can reach her. Daren drops beside her in a heartbeat, fingers pressed against her neck, and signals that she has a steady pulse.

choice("Continue", Ch11a-Poster, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Poster)


ID: Ch11a-Poster
TEXT:

 "Where did that light come from?" Hadrik demands. "Those two casters?"

"Almost certainly," Illuna says, her bright sapphire eyes scanning the alley. "Flower has fallen dormant within our shared mind, and I cannot rouse her. Her soul is locked in stasis, similar to the ward I placed on her in the ogre fort."

As Daren tends to Rose, two robed figures step out from behind the timber corner. One wears black vestments, a coiled serpent tattoo gleaming across his shaved skull. The other wears forest-green robes and a silver circlet.

I recognize them instantly: Zack and the fate-mage, the cultists of Death and Fate who ambushed us with the stillwater skeletons the day we met Hadrik.

"So much for your foolproof talisman, Zack," the green-robed mage sneers. "All that gold, and we knocked out the one girl who can't even cast a cantrip."

"Hold your tongue, fool," Zack snaps. "The peddler warned us the stasis talisman has a low threshold against trained mages. And we caught two, not one. Look at the child—the host soul is dormant, leaving the banshee stranded in the flesh."

"You!" Daren roars from the cobblestones, rising with hand on his hilt. "How are you still standing in Thilias after striking Rose? Why hasn't tournament law banished you?"

"I could ask the same of you, healer in armor," the green-robed mage says with a cold smirk. "Didn't your band butcher a Varathian noble yesterday? That is what the bounty states."

He pulls a rolled parchment from his sleeve and snaps it open. Crudely inked likenesses of Daren, Kate, Eiden, and me stare back, beneath bold lettering: WANTED DEAD OR ALIVE — Daren the Healer and Accomplices, for the Murder of the Earl of Ollendor. The reward sum is in Varathian gold script—easily a fortune.

"We stumbled across this posting yesterday while hunting bounties between tournament rounds," the green-robed mage says. "Pure providence."

"Not providence," Zack hisses. "The God of Death granting us retribution for last week's disgrace. This time, we bury you all in his name."

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Clones, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Clones)
